# ─── Required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These two values are generated by anubix-web when you click
# "Connect my computer". Paste them here.
ANUBIX_INSTALL_TOKEN=
BRIDGE_API_KEY=

# ─── Claude ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Use CLI + your Claude Code / Claude Pro subscription (recommended for Anubix).
# The bridge still uses the Claude Agent SDK; it talks to Anthropic through your
# local `claude` binary, not an API key in this file.
CLAUDE_MODE=cli
# On `npm run dev`, the log line "Claude Code CLI (same as anubix-web..." shows
# the exact binary version anubix-web will use on this machine.
#
# Do NOT put ANTHROPIC_API_KEY here for day-to-day use — easy to leak from git or
# screenshots, and it switches you to billed API usage. Leave it unset and use cli.
# (Advanced: sdk mode exists in code but is not the default product path.)

# ─── Tunnel (how the internet reaches your laptop) ─────────────
# Option A — Named tunnel (stable URL, recommended):
#   Create a Cloudflare tunnel once:  cloudflared tunnel create my-tunnel
#   Then set these two values. npm run dev handles the rest.
# TUNNEL_NAME=my-tunnel
# PUBLIC_URL=https://bridge.example.com
#
# Option B — Quick tunnel (random URL, zero setup):
#   Leave TUNNEL_NAME blank. A random trycloudflare.com URL is
#   generated on every start and registered with anubix-web automatically.
#
# Option C — No tunnel (you manage your own):
#   TUNNEL_MODE=none

# ─── Local paths ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Folder that contains your repos. Claude can open any repo inside it.
# REPOS_BASE_PATH=C:\Users\you\Documents\GitHub

# If cloudflared logs ERR "failed to accept incoming stream" (common with VPNs /
# NordLynx), try forcing HTTP/2 to Cloudflare instead of QUIC:
# CLOUDFLARED_PROTOCOL=http2

# ─── Advanced (rarely needed) ─────────────────────────────────
# PORT=3456
# ANUBIX_WEB_URL=https://anubix.ai

# ─── How to verify everything is correct ─────────────────────
# 1. Run: npm run dev
# 2. Read the block "[bridge] effective settings" — it must match this file.
# 3. Same check over HTTPS (needs your BRIDGE_API_KEY from this file):
#    curl -s -H "x-api-key: YOUR_KEY" https://YOUR_PUBLIC_URL/_bridge/health
#    The JSON "settings" object should match what you expect (tunnel plan,
#    PUBLIC_URL, anubixWebUrl, claudeMode).
# 4. BRIDGE_API_KEY in anubix-web / the browser must equal BRIDGE_API_KEY here.
#    If the UI says "connected" but requests 401, the keys almost certainly
#    differ (or you have duplicate BRIDGE_API_KEY lines in .env — dotenv
#    uses the *first* one).
